"Attractor flow trees, BPS indices and quivers - INSPIRE (https://inspirehep.net/literature/1668938)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.6 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * 1804.06928 [hep-th] ... Inspired by the split attractor flow conjecture for multi-centered black hole solutions in $N = 2$N=2 supergravity, we propose a formula expressing the BPS index $\\Omega (\\gamma , z)$Ω(γ,z) in terms of ‘attractor indices’ $\\Omega_{\\ast} (\\gamma_i)$Ω∗​(γ i​). ... We show how to compute the contribution of each tree directly in terms of asymptotic data, without having to integrate the attractor flow explicitly.\n\n## Attractor flow trees, BPS indices and quivers\n\n  * Sergei Alexandrov(\n\n    * U. Montpellier, L2C and\n    * St. Petersburg State U.\n\n)\n\n,\n  * Boris Pioline(\n\n    * Paris, LPTHE\n\n)\n\nApr 18, 2018\n\n73 pages\n\nPublished in:\n\n  * Adv.Theor.Math.Phys. 23 (2019) 3, 627-699\n\n  * Published: 2019\n\ne-Print:\n\n  * 1804.06928 [hep-th]\n\nDOI:\n\n  * 10.4310/ATMP.2019.v23.n3.a2\n\nReport numbers:\n\n  * L2C:18-057\n\nView in:\n\n  * HAL Science Ouverte,\n  * AMS MathSciNet,\n  * ADS Abstract Service\n\npdfcite claim\n\nreference search37 citations\n\n### Citations per year\n\nAbstract: (International Press)\n\nInspired by the split attractor flow conjecture for multi-centered black hole solutions in $N = 2$N=2 supergravity, we propose a formula expressing the BPS index $\\Omega (\\gamma , z)$Ω(γ,z) in terms of ‘attractor indices’ $\\Omega_{\\ast} (\\gamma_i)$Ω∗​(γ i​). The latter count BPS states in their respective attractor chamber. This formula expresses the index as a sum over stable flow trees weighted by products of attractor indices. We show how to compute the contribution of each tree directly in terms of asymptotic data, without having to integrate the attractor flow explicitly. Furthermore, we derive new representations for the index which make it manifest that discontinuities associated to distinct trees cancel in the sum, leaving only the discontinuities consistent with wall-crossing. We apply these results in the context of quiver quantum mechanics, providing a new way of computing the Betti numbers of quiver moduli spaces, and compare them with the Coulomb branch formula, clarifying the relation between attractor and single-centered indices.\n\nNote:\n\n  * 36+17 pages, 9 figures; v2: figure 4 fixed, to appear in ATMP\n\n  * flow: attractor\n  * supergravity: 2\n  * quiver\n  * BPS\n  * quantum mechanics\n  * moduli space\n  * black hole\n  * stability\n  * Coulomb--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Vardanyan_G in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AVardanyan_G+in%3Acs)\nciteturn8search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ~Vardanyan}, title = {{O}n two consequences of {B}erge-{F}ulkerson conjecture}, year = {2018}, eprint = {1805.06828}, note = {arXiv:1805.06828v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We show that the first statement is equivalent to Fan-Raspaud conjecture.\nMay 18 2018 cs.DM math.CO arXiv:1805.06828v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1805.06828, author = {Vahan V.~Mkrtchyan and Gagik N.~Vardanyan}, title = {{O}n two consequences of {B}erge-{F}ulkerson conjecture}, year = {2018}, eprint = {1805.06828}, note = {arXiv:1805.06828v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe classical Berge-Fulkerson conjecture states that any bridgeless cubic graph $G$ admits a list of six perfect matchings such that each edge of $G$ belongs to two of the perfect matchings from the list. In this short note, we discuss two statements that are consequences of this conjecture. We show that the first statement is equivalent to Fan-Raspaud conjecture. We also show that the smallest counter-example to the second one is a cyclically $4$-edge-connected cubic graph.\n\n  * On strongly spanning $k$-edge-colorable subgraphs\n\nVahan V. Mkrtchyan, Gagik N. Vardanyan\n\nJul 26 2011 cs.DM math.CO arXiv:1107.4879v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1107.4879, author = {Vahan V.~Mkrtchyan and Gagik N.~Vardanyan}, title = {{O}n strongly spanning $k$-edge-colorable subgraphs}, year = {2011}, eprint = {1107.4879}, note = {arXiv:1107.4879v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA subgraph $H$ of a multigraph $G$ is called strongly spanning, if any vertex of $G$ is not isolated in $H$, while it is called maximum $k$-edge-colorable, if $H$ is proper $k$-edge-colorable and has the largest size. We introduce a graph-parameter $sp(G)$, that coincides with the smallest $k$ that a graph $G$ has a strongly spanning maximum $k$-edge-colorable subgraph. Our first result offers some alternative definitions of $sp(G)$. Next, we show that $\\Delta(G)$ is an upper bound for $sp(G)$, and then we characterize the class of graphs $G$ that satisfy $sp(G)=\\Delta(G)$. Finally, we prove some bounds for $sp(G)$ that involve well-known graph-theoretic parameters.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFan–Raspaud conjecture for bridgeless cubic graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/376423/fan-raspaud-conjecture-for-bridgeless-cubic-graphs)\nciteturn8search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Fan–Raspaud conjecture for bridgeless cubic graphs ... The Berge–Fulkerson conjecture implies this conjecture, but the Fan–Raspaud conjecture remains open, including in the finite setting discussed in the source. ... The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2206.10975, arXiv:1805.06828, arXiv:1702.07156, arXiv:1601.05762, arXiv:1501.00860, arXiv:1209.4510.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuivers, black holes and attractor indices Boris P (https://www.lpthe.jussieu.fr/~pioline/Seminars/sem_flowtrees_Warsaw2018.pdf)\nciteturn8search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Quivers, black holes and attractor indices Boris Pioline Conference \"Quantum fields, knots a